WEBVTT

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:01.638
My name is Camo.
我叫卡莫 (Camo)。

00:00:01.638 --> 00:00:02.979
I'm from Istero, Florida.
我来自佛罗里达州伊斯泰罗。

00:00:02.979 --> 00:00:05.339
What does how I make your mother mean to me?
《老爸老妈浪漫史》对我意味着什么？

00:00:05.339 --> 00:00:06.624
It means a lot actually.
其实它意义重大。

00:00:06.624 --> 00:00:08.765
I started watching it early high school.
我上高中早期就开始看了。

00:00:08.765 --> 00:00:10.211
I fell in love with it man.
我简直爱上它了，伙计。

00:00:10.211 --> 00:00:11.924
Like I've watched it ever since.
就像我从那以后一直在看一样。

00:00:11.924 --> 00:00:12.941
I'm in college now.
我现在上大学了。

00:00:12.941 --> 00:00:18.187
I really resonated with a lot of the characters in the show, but mostly Ted and his music choices.
我对剧中的很多角色都很有共鸣，但主要是泰德和他的音乐品味。

00:00:18.187 --> 00:00:27.929
I noticed you know like a lot of the times when Ted falls in love it's George Harrison will play and you know he loves Otis Redding so sometimes like cigarettes and coffee will play.
我注意到，你知道的，很多时候当泰德陷入爱河时，会播放乔治·哈里森的歌，而且你知道他很喜欢奥蒂斯·雷丁，所以有时也会播放《香烟与咖啡》。

00:00:27.929 --> 00:00:30.338
It's like he had this soundtrack to his life.
就好像他的人生有这样一张原声大碟。

00:00:30.338 --> 00:00:33.335
And I remember I showed up to my entrepreneurship class.
我记得我有一次去上创业课。

00:00:33.335 --> 00:00:36.708
They asked us, you know, what do you want to do with your life?
他们问我们，你知道的，你这一生想做些什么？

00:00:36.708 --> 00:00:38.849
I knew in that moment that it was music.
那一刻我知道，那就是音乐。

00:00:38.849 --> 00:00:42.168
You know, I wanted to give people a soundtrack to their lives.
你知道的，我想给人们的生活提供一首原声配乐。

00:00:42.168 --> 00:00:43.452
I wanted to help people.
我想帮助人们。

00:00:43.452 --> 00:00:48.324
You know, it's the reason I started a record label this year, you know, from the ground up.
你知道的，这就是我今年创立唱片公司的原因，你知道的，从零开始。

00:00:48.324 --> 00:00:51.268
Me and a bunch of people, a bunch of nerds about music.
我和一群人，一群对音乐痴迷的极客。

00:00:51.268 --> 00:00:59.940
So I guess I love how I met your mother for a lot of reasons, but it definitely taught me how to care about what you love and not be ashamed to nerd out about it.
所以我想我喜欢《老爸老妈浪漫史》有很多原因，但它绝对教会了我如何去在乎你所爱的事物，并且不要为对它疯狂入迷而感到羞耻。

00:01:00.259 --> 00:01:02.780
And if you surround yourself with the right people, they'll support it.
如果你身边都是对的人，他们会支持你的。

00:01:03.179 --> 00:01:04.939
So hope this reaches you guys.
所以希望这段话能传达到你们那里。

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:06.579
Big fan of the show.
我是该剧的超级粉丝。

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:07.859
Have a great day.
祝你们有美好的一天。

00:01:28.489 --> 00:01:35.090
Hello and welcome to another episode of how we made your mother, where I, Joshua Adner, sit with my very good friend, Craig Thomas.
大家好，欢迎收听《老爸老妈浪漫史》幕后访谈的又一期节目，我是乔舒亚·雷德纳，和我坐在一起的是我的好朋友克雷格·托马斯。

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:35.930
Hi, Craig.
嗨，克雷格。

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:36.769
Hey, they're Josh.
嗨，乔什。

00:01:36.769 --> 00:01:40.969
And we talk about a TV show that we made now many years ago.
我们在这里聊一部我们很多年前制作的电视剧。

00:01:40.969 --> 00:01:42.489
Together, it was called How I Met Your Mother.
我们一起制作的，它叫《老爸老妈浪漫史》。

00:01:42.489 --> 00:01:46.689
It ran on CBS from 2005 to 2014.
它于 2005 年到 2014 年在 CBS 播出。

00:01:46.689 --> 00:01:49.969
I played Ted Mosby on that show, The Eye and How I Met Your Mother.
我在剧中饰演泰德·莫斯比，也就是《老爸老妈浪漫史》里的那个“我”。

00:01:49.969 --> 00:01:53.890
And Craig co-created the show with his college bud, Carter Bayes.
克雷格和他的大学死党卡特·贝斯共同创作了这部剧。

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:59.329
And yeah, we just heard an awesome voice note from.
是的，我们刚刚听到了一段很棒的语音留言。

00:01:59.329 --> 00:02:00.090
I loved that.
我太喜欢了。

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:01.250
That was so great.
真是太棒了。

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:02.609
That was so great.
真是太棒了。

00:02:02.609 --> 00:02:08.310
I feel, the music part is such a big part of the show and such a big part of our bond and Carter's bond with you.
我觉得，音乐部分是这部剧非常重要的一部分，也是我们之间以及卡特和你之间纽带的重要组成部分。

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:12.810
Like music really was, we all liked the same stuff, right?
音乐真的很……我们都喜欢一样的东西，对吧？

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:15.330
It really put us on the same page right away in music.
它确实让我们在音乐上立刻达成了一致。

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:19.789
So the fact that he, music speaks to this, to that guy is like the best.
所以他，音乐能触动这个人、那个家伙，这简直是太棒了。

00:02:19.789 --> 00:02:20.629
I love that.
我很喜欢。

00:02:20.629 --> 00:02:33.389
You know, we could, we really need to have Andy Gowen on the show, our music supervisor who also incidentally was the music supervisor on both of my movies on Happy, Thank You, More, Please and Liberal Arts, just because we had had this great music vibe going already.
你知道的，我们真的该请安迪·高恩上节目，他是我们的音乐总监，顺便说一句，他也是我的两部电影《快乐thank you more please》和《大学同学》的音乐总监，只因为我们之间已经有了这种绝佳的音乐默契。

00:02:33.389 --> 00:02:34.389
Yeah.
是的。

00:02:34.389 --> 00:02:35.389
Yeah.
是的。

00:02:35.389 --> 00:02:52.729
But speaking of music, I mean, you could really, if you are a music fan, I think how I met your mother has a lot of delights, you know, a lot of like classic old songs that you would definitely know and a lot of new kind of ear worms that we broke, you know, some bands on the show.
不过说到音乐，我是说，如果你是一个音乐迷，我认为《老爸老妈浪漫史》有很多让人愉悦的地方，你知道的，有很多你肯定知道的经典老歌，还有很多那种新的洗脑神曲，我们在剧中也发掘了一些乐队。

00:02:52.729 --> 00:03:09.096
You guys were always so great about, I would play you some stuff and some of it really caught you and you put it on the show and and I got to become friends with the bands and the artists and I was also when I joined Twitter, I started doing a song of the day.
你们总是那么棒，我会放给你们听一些东西，其中一些真的吸引了你们，你们把它放进了剧里，然后我有机会和这些乐队和艺术家成为朋友，而且自我加入推特以来，我开始做“每日一歌”。

00:03:09.096 --> 00:03:11.425
Remember I used to do that for years?
还记得我以前那样做好多年吗？

00:03:11.425 --> 00:03:11.925
Yeah.
是的。

00:03:11.925 --> 00:03:13.625
You should bring that back.
你应该把它重新弄回来。

00:03:13.625 --> 00:03:15.262
I'm not on Twitter though.
不过我没用推特。

00:03:15.262 --> 00:03:17.276
I'm not on Twitter, RIP Twitter.
我没用推特，推特安息吧。

00:03:17.276 --> 00:03:20.927
But in this episode, it ends with just jumping to the end.
不过在本集中，直接跳到结尾。

00:03:20.927 --> 00:03:24.956
We're doing Ted Mosby Architect today, episode four, season two.
我们今天要聊的是第二季第四集《泰德·莫斯比，建筑师》。

00:03:24.956 --> 00:03:27.348
Alec, tell us when this episode aired.
亚历克，告诉我们这一集是什么时候播出的。

00:03:27.348 --> 00:03:32.195
This episode was written by Kristin Newman and it aired on October 9th, 2006.
这一集由克里斯汀·纽曼编剧，于 2006 年 10 月 9 日播出。

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:36.610
Well, almost exactly 19, it's October 19 years ago.
嗯，差不多整整 19 年前，是 19 年前的 10 月。

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:39.289
And we're basically sitting here on that day.
我们基本上就在那一天坐在这里。

00:03:39.289 --> 00:03:40.449
We're recording right around then.
我们大约就是在那个时候录制的。

00:03:40.449 --> 00:03:42.250
So symmetry.
所以很有对称感。

00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:49.050
Well, and this episode just just jumping to the end ends with a song by the Decemberists called Here I Dremped I Was an Architect.
嗯，这一集——直接跳到结尾——以十二月党人乐队的一首歌结束，歌名叫《我曾梦想我是一名建筑师》（Here I Dremped I Was an Architect）。

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:49.569
Is that what it was called?
是叫这个名字吗？

00:03:49.569 --> 00:03:50.370
Yeah.
是的。

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:52.849
Yeah, people loved that song on the show.
是的，大家在剧里都很喜欢那首歌。

00:03:52.849 --> 00:03:55.210
It's it's just it's written for the show.
这首歌是专门为这部剧写的。

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:56.969
It's the perfect placement.
这是一个完美的场景运用。

00:03:56.969 --> 00:03:58.930
I got to give credit where it's due.
该居功时得居功。

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:01.330
Gloria Calderon-Kellett pitched that song.
格洛丽亚·卡尔德隆-凯利推荐了这首歌。

00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:02.169
That was her.
那是她的功劳。

00:04:02.169 --> 00:04:06.729
She had a very cool mix of like, she knew we loved music.
她有一种非常酷的音乐直觉，就像她知道我们热爱音乐一样。

00:04:06.729 --> 00:04:09.449
She was like, here's a bunch of songs that say hymnium to me.
她当时说，这有一堆歌，在我看来就像是在向谁致敬。

00:04:09.449 --> 00:04:10.689
And one of them was that song.
其中一首就是那首歌。

00:04:10.689 --> 00:04:12.449
And I think that's how we got it to put it on there.
我想这就是我们把它放进去的原因。

00:04:12.449 --> 00:04:14.729
So it really could come from anywhere.
所以它真的可能来自任何地方。

00:04:14.729 --> 00:04:16.370
Everyone knew we loved music.
每个人都知道我们热爱音乐。

00:04:16.370 --> 00:04:48.050
And I again, almost like the balance of Ashton song in season one, when I would watch the show or know that it was ending with a song like by the decemberous who at that point I think they were having they were kind of breaking right around them like they had this really big album I think that song is from that record right and you know we got to be at the forefront of that and I and it would it would lead me to go oh my god I can't believe I'm on a sitcom on CBS that has the coolest music it always delighted me.
而且我再次觉得，就像第一季里阿什顿那首歌的平衡一样，当我观看这部剧或者知道它将以一首类似于十二月党人乐队的歌结束时，我想当时他们正处于上升期，他们正要火起来，就像他们有了一张非常棒的专辑，我想那首歌就来自那张唱片，对吧？而且，你知道的，我们能够处于那种潮流的前沿，而且这会让我觉得，天哪，我不敢相信我竟然能参演一部哥伦比亚广播公司（CBS）的情景喜剧，里面有最酷的音乐，这总是让我感到高兴。

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:54.821
Cam I love that you singled out Otis Redding and Georgia Harrison and some of the coolest like timeless kind of classic people.
卡莫，我很高兴你特意提到了奥蒂斯·雷丁和乔治·哈里森，还有一些最酷的、永恒的经典人物。

00:04:54.821 --> 00:05:07.990
We didn't always get to, we didn't have enough money to put those ones on every week, but finding that balance between those big ones and then finding cool, smaller stuff, which at the time of December, it's kind of were a little cool and smaller.
我们并不总能做到，我们没有足够的资金每周都把那些大牌的歌放进去，但是在那些大牌音乐和寻找很酷的、小众的东西之间找到平衡——当时十二月党人乐队有点小众而且很酷。

00:05:08.230 --> 00:05:23.389
Now kind of have like a timeless classic feel to them, but there's something also about what he noticed around needle drops for Ted on these big moments were these swelling kind of classic, you know, classic voices, classic artists.
现在它们有一种永恒的经典感觉，但关于他在这些重大时刻为泰德设定的插曲，也有某种特别之处，那就是这些高潮迭起的、某种经典的、你知道的，经典的嗓音，经典的艺术家。

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:47.285
I think the thing about Ted that I always tried to hold on to was I Wanted to believe I was playing a character who would age well But both the character would age well and he would literally I wanted him to age well, you know, you know, okay But there's something about using those classic artists as needle drops to signify These big moments were were like almost outside the bounds of time in space.
我认为关于泰德，我一直试图坚持的一点是：我想相信我扮演的是一个会随着时间沉淀而变得越来越有魅力的角色，不仅角色本身会随着时间沉淀，而且他字面意义上——我希望他能随着时间沉淀，你知道的，你知道的，好吧。但使用这些经典艺术家作为插曲来象征这些重大时刻，有一种奇妙之处，就好像它们几乎超出了时空的界限。它们只是——是的，是的。

00:05:47.285 --> 00:05:48.769
They were just yeah Yeah.
没错。

00:05:48.769 --> 00:05:56.250
But Cam's right, the way that Cam took it is so cool, he says that we're in Ted's shoes when we're hearing those songs.
但卡莫是对的，卡莫的理解方式太酷了，他说当我们听到这些歌时，我们就像是走进了泰德的内心世界。

00:05:56.250 --> 00:06:00.930
Cam's feeling is we're in Ted's mind and soul when we're hearing those songs.
卡莫的感受是，当我们听到这些歌时，我们就在泰德的思想和灵魂里。

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:02.449
Those are Ted's songs.
那是泰德的歌。

00:06:02.449 --> 00:06:03.449
And I like that.
我喜欢这样。

00:06:03.449 --> 00:06:04.449
That's cool.
这很酷。

00:06:04.449 --> 00:06:11.290
It is a story being told by Future Ted, and somehow these songs in some metaphysical way are part of Future Ted's brain and soul.
这确实是一个由未来的泰德讲述的故事，不知何故，这些歌曲以某种形而上学的方式成为了未来的泰德的大脑和灵魂的一部分。

00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:12.370
And that's what it said to Cam.
这就是它对卡莫所意味着的。

00:06:12.370 --> 00:06:13.370
I kind of love that.
我有点喜欢这个观点。

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:15.129
I also love where he took it.
我也很喜欢他引申出的观点。

00:06:15.129 --> 00:06:22.689
He said, the show taught him it was okay to love things, to okay to double down on them and be dorky, like go really deep into it.
他说，这部剧教会了他，去热爱事物是可以的，对它们加倍投入并表现得像个极客也是可以的，比如深入去了解它们。

00:06:22.689 --> 00:06:29.970
I mean, I struggled sometimes because I felt like sometimes Ted's enthusiasm was slapped around by the gang.
我是说，有时我也会挣扎，因为我觉得有时候泰德的热情会遭到伙伴们的嘲讽。

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:31.730
But it never- At times.
但从来没有——有时会有。

00:06:31.730 --> 00:06:38.370
Was it Carter was reading like a Teddy Roosevelt biography and then suddenly Ted was obsessed with Teddy Roosevelt?
是卡特在读一本西奥多·罗斯福的传记，然后泰德突然就迷上了西奥多·罗斯福吗？

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:39.209
Right?
对吧？

00:06:39.209 --> 00:06:40.050
Yeah.
是的。

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:42.689
Right, like he was reading those like doorstop books.
没错，就像他在读那些厚厚的大部头书一样。

00:06:42.689 --> 00:06:44.529
That is absolutely true.
这绝对是真的。

00:06:44.529 --> 00:06:54.610
Carter would proudly admit to it, and Carter was very good at getting very obsessed with something, and it grabbed him, and it had to go on the show.
卡特会自豪地承认这一点，卡特非常擅长对某件事极其着迷，一旦它迷住了他，就必须出现在剧里。

00:06:54.610 --> 00:06:57.370
Often through my character.
通常是通过我的角色。

00:06:57.370 --> 00:06:58.970
Through your character, yeah.
通过你的角色，是的。

00:06:58.970 --> 00:07:03.730
And that was like the catharsis of getting to let go of that move on to the next obsession.
那就像是一种宣泄，得以放下那种情绪，然后转向下一个痴迷的事物。

00:07:03.730 --> 00:07:14.529
So I'm sure you remember this, because the first couple of seasons, this is back when we were still burning CDs to make mixes for people, which felt like the height of new tech cut against technology.
所以我确信你还记得这个，因为在前几季的时候，那还是我们仍在刻录CD来制作混音带送给别人的年代，感觉这就像是对抗科技潮流的科技巅峰。

00:07:14.529 --> 00:07:16.329
I can't believe we can make our own CDVs.
我真不敢相信我们能制作自己的CDV。

00:07:16.329 --> 00:07:17.329
Yeah.
是啊。

00:07:17.329 --> 00:07:18.329
Nothing's ever going to top this technology.
没有什么技术能超越这个了。

00:07:18.329 --> 00:07:22.569
So every couple of weeks, I would burn CDs for the cast.
所以每隔几周，我就会为演员们刻录CD。

00:07:22.569 --> 00:07:26.730
And I think that I know Ali and Kobe really loved it.
而且我觉得我知道阿里和科比真的很喜欢。

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:27.730
I think Jason liked it.
我觉得杰森也喜欢。

00:07:27.730 --> 00:07:30.170
I never knew if Neil liked it, to be honest.
老实说，我从来不知道尼尔是否喜欢。

00:07:30.170 --> 00:07:33.490
But I would give them all an episode.
但我会给他们每人发一张。

00:07:33.490 --> 00:07:34.490
What was the one?
那是哪一个？

00:07:34.490 --> 00:07:35.490
Zip, zip, zip.
Zip, zip, zip。

00:07:35.490 --> 00:07:38.089
Does he make Victoria mixed called Tedscapes?
他做过叫维多利亚混音带的“泰德风景”吗？

00:07:38.089 --> 00:07:40.279
Oh, man.
哦，天哪。

00:07:40.279 --> 00:07:41.279
Please fans help us.
请粉丝们帮帮我们。

00:07:41.279 --> 00:07:42.560
I'm not sure if it's in zip, zip, zip.
我不确定它是不是在《Zip, zip, zip》里。

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:43.879
I think that's a different thing.
我觉得那是另一回事。

00:07:43.879 --> 00:07:45.819
There's something called Tedscapes.
有个东西叫“泰德风景”。

00:07:45.819 --> 00:07:47.160
There's definitely Tedscapes, yeah.
绝对有“泰德风景”，是的。

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:51.240
My friend Jeremiah is staying at my house.
我的朋友杰里米亚正住在我的房子里。

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:53.199
I still have in Los Angeles.
我在洛杉矶的房子。

00:07:53.199 --> 00:08:01.800
And he was looking for some stuff through the garage and he just sent me a photo of a Tedscapes mix that he found in the garage that I'll send to you.
他在车库里找东西，然后刚给我发了一张他在车库里找到的“泰德风景”混音带的照片，我会发给你。

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:02.639
Maybe we'll maybe.
也许我们会……也许吧。

00:08:02.639 --> 00:08:05.319
And you meaning it was on the show and then you started calling your mixes.
你的意思是它出现在剧里，然后你开始把你的混音带叫做这个。

00:08:05.319 --> 00:08:06.920
You would make Tedscapes early.
你早期会制作“泰德风景”。

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:07.759
Oh, yeah, yeah.
哦，对，对。

00:08:07.759 --> 00:08:13.279
Tedscapes was on the show And then I called one of the mixes, Tedscapes.
“泰德风景”出现在剧里，然后我把其中一张混音带叫做“泰德风景”。

00:08:13.279 --> 00:08:16.279
I remember getting handed Tedscapes by you, that was a sweetie.
我记得你递给我过“泰德风景”，那太贴心了。

00:08:16.279 --> 00:08:22.199
Yeah, but that was how me, you, Andy, all became like music buddies on the show.
对，但这就是我和你、安迪如何成为剧组里的音乐死党的方式。

00:08:22.199 --> 00:08:23.040
We were just like.
我们当时就像……

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:23.879
We're music nerds.
我们是音乐迷。

00:08:23.879 --> 00:08:25.160
We're about exactly what Camp said.
我们完全符合坎普所说的那样。

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:29.160
And Andy started including me in the music drops.
然后安迪开始把我加入到音乐分享链里。

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:34.480
He would give you like, once a week, he would give you like a awesome playlist of like new music.
他会给你，一周一次，给你一个很棒的新音乐播放列表。

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:35.375
Every episode.
每一集都这样。

00:08:35.375 --> 00:08:36.080
Yeah, yeah.
对，对。

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:40.720
And you said, I want to be on that chain because I want to hear cool new stuff And that's Andy Gavin's job is to find cool new stuff.
然后你说，我也想加入那个群，因为我想听酷炫的新东西，而安迪·加文的工作就是寻找酷炫的新东西。

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:41.559
And he did it.
他也确实做到了。

00:08:41.559 --> 00:08:45.840
Okay, so this episode is also a mystery episode.
好的，所以这一集也是一集悬疑剧。

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:49.679
It starts off with a WTF, what's going on?
它以一个“搞什么鬼，到底怎么回事”开始。

00:08:49.679 --> 00:08:51.720
This is crazy, right?
这太疯狂了，对吧？

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:54.919
Give us a little summary of what happens in this episode, Craig.
克雷格，给我们简单总结一下这一集里发生了什么。

00:08:54.919 --> 00:08:58.840
Yeah, this episode is diving into Ted and Robin's early.
对，这一集深入探讨了泰德和罗宾的早期阶段。

00:08:58.840 --> 00:09:00.440
It's their first fight, right?
这是他们的第一次争吵，对吧？

00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:02.120
This is their first big problem.
这是他们的第一个大问题。

00:09:03.080 --> 00:09:06.759
Robin thinks to, Robin doesn't know how to be in a couple.
罗宾认为，罗宾不知道如何融入一段情侣关系。

00:09:06.759 --> 00:09:18.879
and she doesn't want to listen to Ted's work complaints, which of course is a huge part of being in a couple, is being able to complain and be complained too, and it's a two-way complaint street and she just doesn't want to deal with it.
她不想听泰德抱怨工作，而这当然是情侣关系中非常重要的一部分，那就是能够抱怨和倾听抱怨，这是一条双向的抱怨街道，而她就是不想应付。

00:09:18.879 --> 00:09:22.399
She's kind of phoning in some aspects of what it takes to be in a couple.
她在维系情侣关系所需付出的努力上有点敷衍。

00:09:22.399 --> 00:09:34.840
And the journey of the episode is her thinking that Ted has stormed off and is perhaps cheating on her using his hot job as an architect as a way to do that.
这一集的旅程是她以为泰德怒气冲冲地离开，并且可能利用他作为建筑师的热门工作作为借口来对她不忠。

00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:37.600
And it's, I like the journey for Robin in this one.
而且，我挺喜欢罗宾在这集里的心路历程的。

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:42.519
She kind of realizes the stakes of being in a relationship and that you do have to go a little crazy.
她有点意识到身处恋爱关系中的利害关系，并且你确实必须表现得有点疯狂。

00:09:42.519 --> 00:09:44.080
You do have to be vulnerable.
你确实必须敞开心扉。

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:45.919
And of course, there's a huge twist to Roo.
当然，对罗宾来说还有一个巨大的反转。

00:09:45.919 --> 00:09:47.919
And we read, it's a classic how I met your mother.
我们看到，这是典型的《老爸老妈浪漫史》。

00:09:47.919 --> 00:09:49.720
We see the story twice kind of.
我们差不多看了两次这个故事。

00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:53.039
We see the story one way that Robin's imagining it.
我们以罗宾想象的一种方式看到了这个故事。

00:09:53.039 --> 00:09:57.120
And then the real way, which is that Barney is posing his head.
然后是真实的方式，那就是巴尼其实是在摆姿势。

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:00.320
And it is, it's a mystery, like many a great how I met your mother episode.
而且，这确实是一个谜，就像许多精彩的《老爸老妈浪漫史》剧集一样。

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:06.879
It's remarkable to me in this rewatch How many of these episodes are a little mystery within the larger mystery?
在这次重看中，让我震惊的是，有多少集在更大的谜团中还包含一个小谜团？

00:10:06.879 --> 00:10:11.519
Like every second or third episode is somehow a weird little mystery.
就像每两三集就会有某种奇怪的小谜团。

00:10:11.519 --> 00:10:12.720
Like what's going on?
比如到底怎么回事？

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:16.720
It's also like a time honored narrative technique.
这也算是一种历史悠久的叙事技巧。

00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:17.559
Yeah.
对。

00:10:17.559 --> 00:10:24.519
You know, every chapter of Harry Potter ends with like a weird cliffhanger that makes you like absolutely have to turn the page.
你知道，《哈利·波特》的每一章都以一个奇怪的悬念结束，让你绝对不得不翻页。

00:10:24.519 --> 00:10:25.360
Definitely.
确实。

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:27.919
I'm reading it out loud with my daughter right now.
我现在正和我女儿大声朗读它。

00:10:27.919 --> 00:10:32.440
And we got to the cliffhanger and she got so worked up she had to run around the room.
我们看到了那个悬念，她激动得不得不在房间里跑来跑去。

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:33.919
She just ran around the room.
她就在房间里跑来跑去。

00:10:33.919 --> 00:10:35.600
I said to Rebecca, we were reading it all.
我对丽贝卡说，我们一直在读它。

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:36.440
We read it as a family.
我们作为一家人一起读。

00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:37.440
We switched off to the voices.
我们换着角色模仿声音。

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:39.120
And still he was like, ah!
即便如此，他还是像：啊！

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:39.960
Like ran around the room.
就像在房间里跑来跑去。

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:40.799
He was like, oh my God.
他当时就像，哦天哪。

00:10:40.799 --> 00:10:45.120
I said, Jesus, I want to write something someday that makes someone run around a fucking room.
我对丽贝卡说，天哪，我希望有一天能写出能让某人在他妈的房间里跑起来的东西。

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:46.559
Cause she was, she's nine years old.
因为她当时，她才九岁。

00:10:46.559 --> 00:10:48.360
She's like, ah, she was running fast.
她就像，啊，她跑得飞快。

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:49.679
She just had to throw off the energy.
她只是不得不把这股能量发泄出来。

00:10:49.679 --> 00:10:50.519
There was just too much.
实在是太多了。

00:10:50.519 --> 00:10:52.200
Yeah, she was like, I've got to get this out somehow.
是的，她当时就像，我得把这股劲儿释放出来。

00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:54.879
She knew she wasn't going to get to go past the cliffhanger.
她知道自己不能跨过那个悬念继续往下读。

00:10:54.879 --> 00:10:55.720
She had to go to bed.
她必须去睡觉了。

00:10:55.720 --> 00:10:56.559
I mean, God bless it.
我是说，老天保佑。

00:10:56.559 --> 00:10:59.519
If you can write something that does that kind of create.
如果你能写出那种能产生这种效果的作品……

00:10:59.519 --> 00:11:03.519
I immediately said that I said, I don't think I've ever written anything that would make someone run around.
我立刻就这么说了，我说，我觉得我从来没有写过能让任何人跑起来的东西。

00:11:03.519 --> 00:11:05.919
I did that with your book, Craig.
我用你的书做到过，克雷格。

00:11:05.919 --> 00:11:07.000
Thank you, Josh.
谢谢你，乔什。

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:10.600
I kept interrupting the audio recording because I kept running around the booth.
我一直在打断音频录制，因为我一直在录音室里跑来跑去。

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:12.159
Yeah.
是的。

00:11:12.159 --> 00:11:22.169
And I wrote you this little day to send you in New York City.
我给你写了这个小日子，要在纽约市寄给你。

00:11:22.169 --> 00:11:32.789
We'll be right back.
我们马上回来。

00:11:32.789 --> 00:11:35.059
And now, back to the show.
现在，回到节目中。

00:11:35.059 --> 00:11:39.299
I just realized I saw my friend, you know Michael Cherness, my friend Michael Cherness.
我刚刚意识到我看到了我的朋友，你知道迈克尔·切尔纳斯吗，我的朋友迈克尔·切尔纳斯。

00:11:39.299 --> 00:12:13.000
He's a great actor, he's on Severance, he was on Origin New Black, he's a long-time friend, we were in drama school at the same time together and he, I saw him in a great play in Fort Green and the gist of the play was this man had died under very strange circumstances and his wife for most of the play doesn't know how to grieve because they were so suspiciously weird and he was with this young college girl and she thinks he was having an affair and the play is done.
他是个伟大的演员，他出演了《人生切割术》，他出演了《女子监狱》，他是多年的老朋友，我们曾经同时在戏剧学校学习，我看到他在福特格林出演了一部很棒的戏剧，这部剧的大意是这个男人在非常奇怪的情况下去世了，他的妻子在剧的大部分时间里都不知道该如何悲伤，因为他们的事情可疑地诡异，他和这个年轻的大学女孩在一起，她以为他在搞外遇，而这出戏就这样演完了。

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:22.159
So I'm not going to even tell you what it is, but the reversal is it turns out he was actually being an extra good guy and someone else was having an affair with her.
所以我甚至不会告诉你那是什么，但反转是，事实证明他其实是个格外好的人，而另一个人在和她外遇。

00:12:22.159 --> 00:12:26.360
And it's revealed that he was actually like the person she thought he was.
并且揭示出他实际上就是她所认为的那种人。

00:12:26.360 --> 00:12:36.440
She spends the whole play wondering who was I married to, and then it turns out she was married to the great beautiful kind man that she thought she was married to.
她整场戏都在想我嫁给了一个什么样的人，然后结果证明她嫁给了她以为自己嫁给的那个伟大、美丽、善良的男人。

00:12:36.440 --> 00:12:42.559
And I think that there's a lot, there's a lot of narrative techniques of, oh, the person you thought was good was actually had a heart of darkness.
而且我认为这里有很多，有很多叙事技巧，哦，你以为是好人的那个人实际上内心深处有一颗黑暗的心。

00:12:42.559 --> 00:12:45.480
And it's a journey into their secret life, right?
这是一次进入他们秘密生活的旅程，对吧？

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:47.200
That's like a trope.
这就像是一个老套的桥段。

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:49.080
But there's something nice about that play.
但那部剧里有些东西还是蛮美好的。

00:12:49.080 --> 00:12:52.600
And this one where it's like, no, he's actually who you think he is.
还有这一幕，就好像是，不，他其实就是你想的那样。

00:12:52.600 --> 00:12:53.600
Yeah.
对。

00:12:53.600 --> 00:12:54.600
This was a misunderstanding.
这其实是一场误会。

00:12:54.600 --> 00:13:00.799
This was, you know, so the reveal, of course, is that everyone saying, oh, Ted Mosby architect, he was here.
这，你知道的，所以真相大白当然是，每个人都在说，哦，泰德·莫斯比，那个建筑师，他来过这里。

00:13:00.799 --> 00:13:02.919
was crazy, and he was seducing everyone.
简直疯了，而且他在勾引所有人。

00:13:02.919 --> 00:13:12.919
And it turns out it's Barney wearing the costume of Ted, saying, I'm Ted Mosby, architect, and being Barney, but with this different cloak on.
结果发现其实是巴尼穿着泰德的衣服，说，我是泰德·莫斯比，建筑师，一边做着巴尼会做的事，但套着这样一件不同的外衣。

00:13:12.919 --> 00:13:18.519
Yeah, and it's Robin's willingness to go a little crazy and then realizing that is what it means to be in a relationship.
对，这就是罗宾愿意稍微疯狂一下，然后意识到这就是身处恋爱中的含义。

00:13:18.519 --> 00:13:22.480
You think you have that other person figured out, he's just gonna come home and complain about work.
你以为你已经把另一个人摸透了，他不过就是回家抱怨一下工作。

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:26.399
And then it's revealed to you that everyone is a mystery, right?
然后真相才向你揭示，每个人都是一个谜，对吧？

00:13:26.399 --> 00:13:29.879
She goes down this rabbit hole of like, maybe I don't know this guy at all.
她陷入了这种胡思乱想，比如，也许我根本就不了解这家伙。

00:13:29.879 --> 00:13:33.720
And it does turn out to be a complete mislead, but it's a good lesson for her to learn.
事实证明这完全是个误导，但对她来说是个很好的教训。

00:13:33.720 --> 00:13:35.840
Like you don't have him figured out.
就好像你并没有真正摸透他。

00:13:35.840 --> 00:13:37.440
You don't know everything about Ted.
你并不了解关于泰德的一切。

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:39.240
It's still early days in this.
这段感情现在还处于早期。

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:47.559
And not only that, the thought that he might be straying and she might be losing him is really hard for her.
不仅如此，一想到他可能会出轨，而她可能会失去他，这让她觉得非常难受。

00:13:47.559 --> 00:13:48.399
Yeah.
对。

00:13:48.399 --> 00:13:56.269
So it's like, it's almost like you don't know what, it's like cliche, you don't know what you got until it's gone, right?
所以这就好像，差不多就像你不知道怎么说，这就跟老套路一样，失去了才知道珍惜，对吧？

00:13:56.269 --> 00:14:08.590
like she starts fantasizing about like, oh, he's going away and I will be bereft and therefore I have to reconceive of who I am in a relationship.
比如她开始幻想，哦，他要走了，我会失去一切，因此我必须重新构想我在恋爱中的身份。

00:14:08.590 --> 00:14:13.529
I think you guys were still really finding the Shurbatski avoid an attachment style.
我觉得你们当时还在摸索舒巴茨基那种逃避型的依恋风格。

00:14:14.669 --> 00:14:18.110
Like second season feels like you really dialed that in more.
感觉到了第二季，你们真的把这个特质拿捏得更准了。

00:14:18.110 --> 00:14:19.230
Yeah, definitely.
对，绝对是的。

00:14:19.230 --> 00:14:21.070
I think that's the gift of a long-running TV show.
我觉得这就是长寿电视剧的馈赠。

00:14:21.070 --> 00:14:29.029
You start to peel back the layers and I love she starts the episode off so cocky And so kind of dismissive of, I don't have to do the bullshit of couplehood.
你开始剥开层层外壳，我特别喜欢她在一开始显得那么傲慢，而且有点不屑一顾，觉得我才不需要情侣之间那些狗屁倒灶的事。

00:14:29.029 --> 00:14:31.789
We'll just get the bullshit part and we'll just do the fun parts.
我们只要拿走那些扯淡的部分，然后只享受有趣的部分就行了。

00:14:31.789 --> 00:14:32.789
You can't do that.
你不能这么做。

00:14:32.789 --> 00:14:33.629
She, you know what I mean?
她，你懂我的意思吗？

00:14:33.629 --> 00:14:35.230
She just, that doesn't work.
她就是，这根本行不通。

00:14:35.230 --> 00:14:38.029
And real watching her spiral and become vulnerable.
而看着她情绪失控并变得脆弱。

00:14:38.029 --> 00:14:46.789
I really liked that moment and how Kobe played it where she says, I took the nicest guy in the world and I turned him into a cheater because I was, I took him for granted.
我真的很喜欢那个时刻以及寇碧的演绎，她说，我把世界上最好的人变成了一个骗子，因为我，我把他的好当作理所当然。

00:14:46.789 --> 00:14:50.629
She's wrong about that, but it's important for her to take the hit on that one.
她在这点上想错了，但对她来说，敢于承认错误是很重要的。

00:14:50.629 --> 00:14:55.629
And that's, you watch Robin grow in this one.
这就是，你会看到罗宾在这一集里的成长。

00:14:56.389 --> 00:14:58.870
You watch her learn something in this one, which I like.
你会看到她在这一集里学到了一些东西，这让我很喜欢。

00:14:58.870 --> 00:15:04.669
I went to this, these two men's retreats with this group called Every Man, E-V-R-Y-M-A-N.
我参加过由一个叫Every Man（E-V-R-Y-M-A-N）的团体举办的两次男性静修活动。

00:15:04.669 --> 00:15:12.590
If there are any men out there who are looking for more kind of like to drop into their feelings and lean in more to community, I can't recommend this highly enough.
如果有哪位男士正在寻找更多能够表达内在感受并更好地融入社群的方式，我强烈推荐这个活动。

00:15:12.590 --> 00:15:18.750
But this group, it spun off a weekly men's group that I do every Monday night.
但这个团体后来衍生出了一个每周一次的男士聚会，我每周一晚上都会参加。

00:15:18.750 --> 00:15:33.379
And we do this thing called checking in, which I love very much, and Jordana loves it so much that we do it in my marriage, which is essentially you say, Josh, checking in, and you say, you describe what you're feeling physically and sensations.
我们会做一个叫做“签到”的环节，我非常喜欢，乔丹娜也特别喜欢，以至于我们在婚姻里也会这样做，基本上就是你说，乔什，签到，然后你要描述你在身体上的感受和感官体验。

00:15:33.379 --> 00:15:34.899
I feel tightness at the top of my shoulders.
我觉得双肩顶部感到紧绷。

00:15:34.899 --> 00:15:36.940
I feel a scratchiness of my throat.
我觉得喉咙有些干痒。

00:15:36.940 --> 00:15:40.259
I feel labored breathing.
我觉得呼吸有些费力。

00:15:40.259 --> 00:15:42.379
And then you say, any emotions you're feeling.
然后你要说出你感受到的任何情绪。

00:15:42.379 --> 00:15:45.779
I feel grateful, I feel anxiety.
我感到感激，我感到焦虑。

00:15:45.779 --> 00:15:48.220
I feel excitement about tonight.
我对今晚感到兴奋。

00:15:48.220 --> 00:15:52.259
and I feel thirsty.
我还觉得口渴。

00:15:52.259 --> 00:15:53.740
And then you say, I'm here and I'm in.
接着你要说，我在这里，我投入了。

00:15:53.740 --> 00:15:59.019
And then the other person says, aho, which is like a kind of Native American I'm in.
然后另一个人会说，Aho，这有点像美洲原住民语里的“我加入了”。

00:15:59.019 --> 00:16:03.659
But we developed something where if you don't really want to check in, all you want to do is complain.
但我们开发了一种新玩法，如果你其实不想签到，只想抱怨的话。

00:16:03.659 --> 00:16:07.299
We call it confetching in instead of checking in.
我们把它叫做“发牢骚签到”（confetching in），而不是普通的签到。

00:16:07.299 --> 00:16:10.299
So it was almost like, can I confet in?
所以就好像是，我能发个牢骚吗？

00:16:10.299 --> 00:16:13.860
And you say, yeah, you go, I'm annoyed about my haircut.
然后对方说，行，你说吧，我开始抱怨我的发型。

00:16:13.860 --> 00:16:15.740
I didn't get enough sleep.
我觉没睡够。

00:16:15.740 --> 00:16:18.059
And you just, it's a litany of complaints.
然后你就不停地数落一堆抱怨。

00:16:18.059 --> 00:16:22.059
And then instead of saying, I'm here and I'm in, you say, I'm here and I'm annoyed.
接着，不用说“我在这里，我投入了”，你要说“我在这里，我很烦躁”。

00:16:22.059 --> 00:16:26.259
And then the other person says, achoo, like a sneeze instead of aho.
然后另一个人会学打喷嚏说“阿嚏”，而不是说“Aho”。

00:16:26.259 --> 00:16:29.740
This is all like deep marriage, you know, inside joke stuff.
这完全是那种深刻的婚姻内部梗。

00:16:29.740 --> 00:16:39.500
But one, the thing I really love about it is, it's like full permission, like take 30 seconds to a minute and complain.
但是有一点，我真的很喜欢它的是，这就像是获得了完全的许可，花30秒到1分钟的时间来抱怨。

00:16:39.500 --> 00:16:42.460
Like you have a runway, complain, tell me what.
就像给你一条跑道，尽管抱怨，告诉我什么。

00:16:42.460 --> 00:16:43.779
And I won't try to fix it.
而且我不会试着去解决它。

00:16:43.779 --> 00:16:45.019
But I like that it's limited.
但我喜欢它是有限制的。

00:16:45.019 --> 00:16:46.019
It's limited, yes.
是的，是有限制的。

00:16:46.019 --> 00:16:48.019
That's what's brilliant about that.
这正是它绝妙的地方。

00:16:48.019 --> 00:16:50.019
Get it out, but don't let it spread.
宣泄出来，但不要让它蔓延。

00:16:50.019 --> 00:16:52.019
I think that's what Robin would say yesterday.
我想这正是罗宾昨天会说的话。

00:16:52.019 --> 00:16:53.019
Yeah, exactly.
对，完全正确。

00:16:53.019 --> 00:16:54.019
There's another...
还有另一个……

00:16:54.019 --> 00:16:57.019
I guess we have...
我想我们有……

00:16:57.019 --> 00:16:59.019
I have a real...
我有一个真正的……

00:16:59.019 --> 00:17:03.269
I like giving people permission to complain up to a point.
我喜欢允许人们在一定程度上抱怨。

00:17:03.269 --> 00:17:05.269
You know what I mean?
你知道我的意思吗？

00:17:05.269 --> 00:17:07.269
We should all be cut off.
我们都应该被叫停。

00:17:07.269 --> 00:17:08.269
There should be a timer on it.
这上面应该设个计时器。

00:17:08.269 --> 00:17:12.269
Because the catharsis of it is normal and natural and good.
因为这种情绪宣泄是正常、自然且有益的。

00:17:12.269 --> 00:17:15.269
But have you ever heard that thing in relationships?
但是你听说过恋爱中的那个说法吗？

00:17:15.269 --> 00:17:23.009
when someone comes to you with a problem and you say, do you need a tissue box, a soap box or a toolbox?
当有人带着问题来找你时，你会说，你需要一盒纸巾、一个肥皂箱还是一个工具箱？

00:17:23.009 --> 00:17:24.490
No, but that's great.
没有，但这个太棒了。

00:17:24.490 --> 00:17:27.609
So it's like tissue box is like, you just wanna like weep and complain.
所以“一盒纸巾”的意思是，你只是想痛哭流涕地抱怨。

00:17:27.609 --> 00:17:28.769
I'll give you a tissue box.
那我给你拿盒纸巾。

00:17:28.769 --> 00:17:32.049
A soap box is you wanna rant and you just wanna rail.
“肥皂箱”的意思是你想破口大骂，宣泄不满。

00:17:32.049 --> 00:17:36.039
And a toolbox is do you want help and advice?
而“工具箱”则是问你需要帮助和建议吗？

00:17:36.039 --> 00:17:36.880
It's good, right?
这很好，对吧？

00:17:36.880 --> 00:17:37.720
It's good.
这很好。

00:17:37.720 --> 00:17:39.200
Have we turned into a self-help podcast today?
我们今天怎么变成了一个心灵成长播客了？

00:17:39.200 --> 00:17:40.720
Cause all of this is fucking gold.
因为这些内容全都是他妈的干货金句。

00:17:40.720 --> 00:17:41.559
I think so.
我想是的。

00:17:41.559 --> 00:17:42.400
I love this.
我太喜欢这个了。

00:17:42.400 --> 00:17:43.240
Yeah.
对。

00:17:43.240 --> 00:17:48.240
Yeah, so it's fascinating to watch Ted and Rod.
对，所以看泰德和罗…很有意思。

00:17:48.599 --> 00:17:58.599
The other thing that relationships, the way you fall in love, we might have talked about this before, the way you fall in love is like, oh my God, she likes the Decemberists.
另一件关于恋爱的事，你坠入爱河的方式，我们以前可能讨论过，你坠入爱河的方式就像是，哦天哪，她喜欢冬日乐队（The Decemberists）。

00:17:58.599 --> 00:18:00.359
I like the Decemberists.
我也喜欢冬日乐队。

00:18:00.359 --> 00:18:01.920
Autumn is her favorite season.
秋天是她最喜欢的季节。

00:18:01.920 --> 00:18:03.480
It's my favorite season.
这也是我最喜欢的季节。

00:18:03.480 --> 00:18:08.640
There's all the like, yes, yes, this lines up, this lines up, we're similar, we're similar, we're similar.
会有各种诸如此类的感觉，没错，没错，这很合拍，这很合拍，我们很相似，我们很相似，我们很相似。

00:18:08.640 --> 00:18:18.880
It's like nature's way of getting you to fall in love, release those chemicals, the bonding happens, and then one day you're like, oh, we actually have some real differences, you know?
这就像是自然促使你坠入爱河的方式，释放那些化学物质，产生羁绊，然后有一天你会觉得，哦，我们之间其实存在一些真正的分歧，你知道吗？

00:18:18.880 --> 00:18:31.279
And that's when a relationship, that's when you really, I think that's a moment of reckoning where you have to say, do I feel strong enough about this person that I'm going to journey through the valley of these differences and figure out how we can be together?
而这就是当一段关系，这就是你真正，我认为这是一个清算时刻，你必须扪心自问，我对这个人有足够的感情，能够穿梭于这些分歧的山谷，并找出我们如何能在一起的方法吗？

00:18:31.279 --> 00:18:34.839
Or is this just not, is this gap not bridgeable?
还是说这根本不行，这道鸿沟无法弥合？

00:18:34.839 --> 00:18:40.680
And I think they're at, they've been together long enough that they're like at that point of like, oh, they're just hitting that point.
我觉得他们在一起的时间够久了，所以他们正处于那种状态，哦，他们刚好碰到了那个节点。

00:18:40.680 --> 00:18:41.680
Yeah.
对。

00:18:41.680 --> 00:18:47.599
What I like about the end of this episode is that Ted kind of went, first of all, she gives a little.
我喜欢这一集结尾的地方在于，泰德有点像是，首先，她做出了一点妥协。

00:18:47.599 --> 00:18:48.599
She gives a little.
她做出了一点让步。

00:18:48.599 --> 00:18:49.960
She says, I'll listen to you complain.
她说，我会听你抱怨。

00:18:49.960 --> 00:18:50.960
She misses him.
她开始想念他了。

00:18:50.960 --> 00:18:51.960
She thought she was losing him.
她以为她要失去他了。

00:18:51.960 --> 00:18:52.960
She comes to him.
她来找他。

00:18:52.960 --> 00:18:55.160
She literally comes to him and she has an offering.
她真的来找他了，而且她带了礼物。

00:18:55.160 --> 00:18:57.039
She has like donuts and coffee.
她带了甜甜圈和咖啡之类的东西。

00:18:57.039 --> 00:18:58.200
She comes and is humble.
她放低姿态过来了。

00:18:58.200 --> 00:19:04.000
But I really like that he switches modes from what I'm complaining about to what I'm excited about.
但我真的很喜欢他转换了频道，从我抱怨的事情转到了让我兴奋的事情上。

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:15.089
And I think that's something that you can get very stuck in sticking with the relationship So if I'll metaphor here, I feel like the complaining will expand to fill the space given to the complaining.
我觉得这是你在维系感情时很容易陷入的一种困境，如果用个隐喻的话，我觉得抱怨会无限膨胀，直到填满分配给它的空间。

00:19:15.089 --> 00:19:18.529
That's why I like the 30 second or the one minute, like the limit.
这就是为什么我喜欢30秒或1分钟的限制。

00:19:18.529 --> 00:19:22.630
And then you also need to offset the complaining by being like, this is what I fucking love.
然后你也需要通过说“这真是我他妈最爱的”来平衡抱怨。

00:19:22.630 --> 00:19:30.890
When Ted shows her that plan for the building, it's a great ending to that episode because it's like, it really uses the future perspective, the future narrator.
当泰德向她展示那个建筑的规划图时，那是那一集非常棒的结尾，因为它真的运用了未来的视角、未来的旁白。

00:19:30.890 --> 00:19:32.329
Ted isn't such a low point.
泰德正处于人生的低谷。

00:19:32.329 --> 00:19:34.170
He feels stuck at this terrible job.
他觉得自己被困在这份糟糕的工作中。

00:19:34.170 --> 00:19:38.849
And yet we get this peak through the narrator that this building's gonna be built.
然而，通过旁白我们得以窥见，这座建筑最终会被建造出来。

00:19:38.849 --> 00:19:44.529
Robin's the first person he shows this dream to, and that dream's gonna come true.
罗宾是他展示这个梦想的第一个人，而那个梦想将成为现实。

00:19:44.529 --> 00:19:47.609
I really liked that moment and their connection and that moment.
我真的很喜欢那个时刻，以及他们之间的连接和那个时刻。

00:19:47.609 --> 00:19:52.289
She comes to him with something and he's able to shift gears out of, she says, I'll listen to you complain.
她带着事情来找他，而他能够转换状态，不再纠结于——她说，我会听你抱怨。

00:19:52.289 --> 00:19:54.089
And he says, no, I don't need that anymore.
他说，不，我不需要那个了。

00:19:54.089 --> 00:19:55.809
Look at my dream.
看看我的梦想。

00:19:55.809 --> 00:19:58.930
Maybe someday this will come true and she says, that's beautiful.
也许有一天这会成真，她说，这太美妙了。

00:19:58.930 --> 00:20:01.009
It's a great moment at the end of this episode.
这是这集结尾的高光时刻。

00:20:01.009 --> 00:20:03.529
It's a great ending scene.
这是一个很棒的结局场景。

00:20:03.529 --> 00:20:06.930
Like it could otherwise be kind of a gimmicky.
否则它可能会变成一个有点耍小聪明的。

00:20:06.930 --> 00:20:11.130
episode, if not for the way you guys landed the plane.
剧集，要不是你们把结局处理得那么好的话。

00:20:11.130 --> 00:20:14.970
Like, it really feels like it's, it was quite moving to me that's it.
真的，我觉得，对我来说这挺感人的，就是这样。

00:20:14.970 --> 00:20:17.569
It was, I forgot how moving the ending of that episode was.
确实是，我忘了那一集的结尾有多感人了。

00:20:17.569 --> 00:20:29.730
I'd really got me, I really blanked, and I was thinking as I was watching it, like, oh, this one right is kind of a late, farcical episode, and I wanna ask you about what it was like to play that, but like, I, the ending really got me.
真的触动到我了，我当时脑子一片空白，看的时候还在想，哦，这一集有点像那种后期的荒诞剧，我想问问你当时演这种感觉是什么样的，但是，这个结局真的打动了我。

00:20:29.730 --> 00:20:31.250
It's Ted's first building.
这是泰德的第一座建筑。

00:20:31.250 --> 00:20:38.289
It's a huge fucking deal that, it's his first huge career win, And he doesn't know it yet, but we get to know it.
这他妈是一件大事，这是他职业生涯中第一个巨大的成功，而且他自己还不知道，但我们知道了。

00:20:38.289 --> 00:20:41.329
And there's something nice about a traumatic irony of that.
这种戏剧性的讽刺有一种很棒的美感。

00:20:41.329 --> 00:21:01.450
And also there's something, I think a very deep lesson in here that even if you're doing what you're passionate about for your career or what you love or what your great talent is, there's still gonna be days of like absolute drudgery, absolute loss of belief in self, loss of belief in the larger kind of, you lose the plot of it.
而且我觉得里面还有一个很深刻的教训：即使你从事的是你充满热情的职业，或是你所爱的事业，或是你极具天赋的领域，依然会有一些日子感到绝对的枯燥、绝对的自我怀疑、对宏大愿景的失去信念，你迷失了方向。

00:21:01.450 --> 00:21:02.369
Same with relationships.
感情也是如此。

00:21:02.369 --> 00:21:08.690
You can be in a great relationship that has rough patches that has, you know, you miss each other sometimes.
你可能会拥有一段美好的感情，但它也会有磕磕绊绊的时候，你知道的，你们有时会错过彼此。

00:21:08.690 --> 00:21:33.849
And I think that, I think that, how I met your mother was really good about not, I mean, in some ways it's a kind of urban fantasy, but at the same time it was really trying to ground itself in the facts of relationships, but the bumpiness of relationships, the almost impossibility of like communicating with people and then finding a way through it.
而且我认为，《老爸老妈浪漫史》真的很擅长——虽然在某些方面它有点像都市童话，但与此同时，它又真的试图立足于感情的现实，感情中的坎坷，与人沟通几乎是不可能的任务，但最终还是找到了解决的办法。

00:21:33.849 --> 00:21:35.730
Yeah, I agree.
是的，我同意。

00:21:35.730 --> 00:21:37.410
That's what was so nice about the end of this one.
这正是这一集结尾最美妙的地方。

00:21:37.410 --> 00:21:38.849
Let me ask you this.
我问你个问题。

00:21:38.849 --> 00:21:52.890
What was it like to play the middle of the episode when we're kind of seeing Robin's perception of her fear of what Ted might be that she didn't realize he was or that he's driven him away, she's driven him to this?
出演中间那段剧情是什么感觉？当时我们看到的是罗宾眼中的恐惧，她害怕泰德可能是某种她以前没意识到的人，或者她把泰德逼成了这样，是他把泰德逼到这一步的？

00:21:52.890 --> 00:21:57.650
I'm always curious, the audience watching this for the first time, did they really buy Ted?
我一直很好奇，第一次看这个节目的观众，他们真的相信泰德吗？

00:21:57.650 --> 00:21:58.970
Was acting that way?
演的时候是那样的吗？

00:21:58.970 --> 00:22:00.730
Did the whole time were you watching it going?
你在看的时候，从头到尾都在想：

00:22:00.730 --> 00:22:02.049
Something's up here.
这里面有什么不对劲吧？

00:22:02.049 --> 00:22:03.410
I'm sure there are both reactions.
我敢说肯定有这两种反应。

00:22:03.410 --> 00:22:07.130
I would, I would wager you kind of know that there's something up his head is acting so differently.
我敢打赌，你多少能猜到有点不对劲，他的状态太反常了。

00:22:07.130 --> 00:22:12.170
Was it fun to lean into playing Barney, playing Ted, but we're seeing your face?
倾向于去扮演尼尔（饰演巴尼）假装的泰德，但我们看到的又是你的脸，这样演好玩吗？

00:22:12.170 --> 00:22:13.170
Like what was that?
那是什么感觉？

00:22:13.170 --> 00:22:14.250
What was that like to play?
那种表演体验是怎样的？

00:22:14.250 --> 00:22:17.970
I'll tell you this when, when Jordana and I watched it, she was like alarmed.
我这么跟你说吧，当乔丹娜（Jordana）和我一起看的时候，她很惊慌。

00:22:17.970 --> 00:22:19.089
She was like, what's going on?
她说，这怎么回事？

00:22:19.089 --> 00:22:20.210
Like why is he doing that?
为什么他要那样做？

00:22:20.210 --> 00:22:21.049
Like she didn't.
她当时没看出来。

00:22:21.049 --> 00:22:23.009
This is the, why didn't I think to ask you about Jordana?
这就是——我怎么就没想到问问乔丹娜的看法呢？

00:22:23.009 --> 00:22:23.849
She bought it.
她相信了。

00:22:23.849 --> 00:22:25.410
I think she eventually got ahead of it.
我想她最终还是反应过来了。

00:22:25.410 --> 00:22:26.970
Like she got on to it.
她看出来了。

00:22:26.970 --> 00:22:31.170
But for a while she was like a little, she was like a little upset.
但有一段时间她觉得有点，她觉得有点难受。

00:22:31.170 --> 00:22:32.809
Like what's going on?
就像是：这到底是怎么回事？

00:22:32.809 --> 00:22:49.339
I will say this, I do remember enjoying immensely playing the rake or playing the, like, kind of, because in a series, you know, everyone is given their, like, notes on the keyboard.
我要说的是，我确实记得非常享受扮演那个放荡不羁的人，或者说扮演那种角色，因为在一部剧里，你知道，每个人都有自己固定的音符。

00:22:49.339 --> 00:22:53.039
And even though I think Ted was a complicated character, it's like, you're still playing in one-off.
尽管我觉得泰德是个复杂的角色，但你还是在弹奏一个固定的单音。

00:22:53.039 --> 00:22:54.039
There's a certain key.
有一个特定的基调。

00:22:54.039 --> 00:22:55.039
You know what I mean?
你知道我的意思吗？

00:22:55.039 --> 00:22:56.039
Like a certain key.
就像一个特定的基调。

00:22:56.039 --> 00:22:57.039
It's headspace.
那是某种思维状态。

00:22:57.039 --> 00:22:58.039
Yeah.
对。

00:22:58.039 --> 00:22:59.039
Yeah.
对。

00:22:59.039 --> 00:23:36.400
So I, whenever I was able to do stuff like whether it was in pineapple, isn't it, you know, where he got to be drunk, anything that gives a little Mr. Hyde to the Dr. Jekyll, I always found it to be really fun because you find yourself hitting those similar notes which you were hired to hit, I knew that I was good and fluent at those notes, I was hired in the band to play that part, but it was fun sometimes when it's like, I don't know, let Ringo sing or like when you mix it up, it feels, it was really fun.
所以每当我能做一些诸如《菠萝快车》（注：此处指老爸老妈浪漫史中经典的凤梨疑云集）里那种喝醉酒的事情时，任何能给“杰基尔博士”加上一点“海德先生”特质的东西，我都觉得特别有趣，因为你会发现自己在弹奏那些你被雇来弹奏的熟悉音符，我知道自己很擅长弹奏这些音符，我被乐队雇来就是为了弹奏这个部分，但有时候如果能像——我不知道，让林戈（Ringo）来主唱，或者当你把风格混合一下，那种感觉，真的非常有趣。

00:23:36.400 --> 00:23:40.960
And I found it, I remember feeling pretty liberated in it.
我发现，我记得当时在其中感到相当解放。

00:23:40.960 --> 00:23:45.279
It's almost like playing a villain can be really fun.
这有点像扮演反派会觉得非常有趣。

00:23:45.279 --> 00:23:48.400
It's like, it's just fun, it's fun to play.
就像是，这很有趣，演起来很有意思。

00:23:48.400 --> 00:23:51.160
And I think Neil had a blast with this for nine straight years.
我想尼尔连续九年都乐在其中。

00:23:51.160 --> 00:24:01.759
Like, it's fun to play socially unacceptable things like in a fictional context, where you then get to go home to your normal life and have dinner afterwards.
扮演那些在社交上不被接受的角色真的很有趣，当然是在虚构的语境中，之后你还可以回家过你的正常生活，吃晚饭。

00:24:01.759 --> 00:24:05.359
And like no one's, you don't get the consequence, you get applause.
而且没人，你不需要承担后果，你收获的是掌声。

00:24:05.359 --> 00:24:06.519
You know?
你知道吗？

00:24:06.519 --> 00:24:15.960
It's funny how the moments you got to do that kind of thing, the Ted got to do that kind of thing, even though this was kind of a fantasy, he wasn't really doing this.
有趣的是，每当你能做那种事情的时候，泰德能做那种事情的时候，尽管这有点像是个幻想，他并不是真的在这样做。

00:24:15.960 --> 00:24:17.960
It's always, it makes you lean forward a little bit.
这总是会让你稍微把身子往前倾一点。

00:24:17.960 --> 00:24:22.559
It's good to keep the audience a little on their toes and be like, what's happening here?
让观众时刻保持一点警惕是件好事，会让他们想：这里到底在发生什么？

00:24:22.559 --> 00:24:25.400
And just see another side of you as an actor.
并看到你作为演员的另一面。

00:24:25.400 --> 00:24:32.480
And then conversely with Neil as Barney, when you saw a little flicker of humanity and warmth from Barney, it was really interesting.
相反，对于饰演巴尼的尼尔来说，当你看到巴尼身上偶尔闪现的人性和温暖时，真的非常有趣。

00:24:32.480 --> 00:24:39.480
It was always, those little opposite grace that the Dr. Declan Mr. Hyde, that you want to see that other side of the coin sometimes.
那种杰基尔博士与海德先生之间的对立美感总是很吸引人，有时你就是想看到硬币的另一面。

00:24:39.480 --> 00:24:40.319
Yeah.
对。

00:24:40.319 --> 00:24:41.160
And it's powerful.
而且这很有冲击力。

00:24:41.160 --> 00:24:53.839
Isn't it also, and I think they've done studies, the reason that like deep fakes are so concerning is because if you see one, it imprints upon you, whether it's true or not, let's just call it untrue.
不是吗，而且我想他们做过研究，深伪（deepfake）技术之所以如此令人担忧，是因为如果你看到了一个，它就会深深印在你的脑海里，不管它是真是假，我们姑且称之为假的。

00:24:53.839 --> 00:24:55.160
It's a deep, it's a deep fake.
这是一个深伪。

00:24:55.160 --> 00:24:58.859
You know, they, politicians saying something they never said.
你知道的，政客们说了一些他们从未说过的话。

00:24:58.859 --> 00:25:02.099
And then when you, when it's been revealed, this was actually doctored.
然后当你，当真相被揭露，这其实是伪造的。

00:25:02.099 --> 00:25:06.140
This was, you go, oh, that wasn't real, but something of it.
这是，你会说，哦，那不是真的，但它的一部分已经留下了。

00:25:06.140 --> 00:25:07.779
Yeah, you still have the memory.
对，你脑子里依然有记忆。

00:25:07.779 --> 00:25:10.180
You still, it's like as if you saw it, right?
你依然，就好像你亲眼看到过一样，对吧？

00:25:10.180 --> 00:25:13.579
Like your, your reptilian brain is like, no, no, no, I saw that happen then.
你的爬行动物脑会觉得：不，不，不，我当时明明看到那个发生了。

00:25:13.579 --> 00:25:14.420
That's true.
那是真的。

00:25:14.420 --> 00:25:18.339
I saw the shocking thing coming out of this person's mouth and it changed my perception of them.
我看到这个人嘴里说出了那些震撼的话，这改变了我对他们的看法。

00:25:18.339 --> 00:25:20.539
That's what this episode kind of is in Robin's brain.
这就是这一集在罗宾脑海里的作用。

00:25:20.539 --> 00:25:21.660
It's kind of what's happening.
某种程度上就是这样发生的。

00:25:21.660 --> 00:25:27.099
And in some ways, Ted, for both the viewer and for Robin, It's like forever changed a little bit.
在某种意义上，泰德，无论是对观众还是对罗宾来说，都有点被永远地改变了。

00:25:27.099 --> 00:25:31.140
Like the idea that like, well, he didn't actually do those things, but he's capable of them.
就好像：好吧，他虽然没有真正做过那些事，但他有能力做出来。

00:25:31.140 --> 00:25:31.980
Or did he do that?
或者说他真的做过吗？

00:25:31.980 --> 00:25:33.299
Like I think I saw him do them.
我觉得我看到他做了。

00:25:33.299 --> 00:25:34.380
I imagine him doing them.
我脑补了他做这些事的画面。

00:25:34.380 --> 00:25:36.180
Other people reported he did them.
别人说他做了。

00:25:37.579 --> 00:25:38.980
Yeah, it's fascinating.
对，这太迷人了。

00:25:38.980 --> 00:25:43.900
It's one thing that really I find unnerving about the future we're throttling towards.
这是让我对我们正全速冲向的未来感到不安的一大原因。

00:25:43.900 --> 00:25:44.900
Oh, yes.
哦，是的。

00:25:44.900 --> 00:25:46.460
I hate it so much.
我太讨厌了。

00:25:46.460 --> 00:25:47.779
It's so bad.
实在是太糟糕了。

00:25:47.779 --> 00:25:50.740
Obviously this episode moves very quickly.
显然这一集节奏非常快。

00:25:50.740 --> 00:25:53.700
And am I right in saying there is no B story.
我说得对吗，这一集没有B线剧情。

00:25:53.700 --> 00:25:56.019
There are sort of two sides to the A store.
A线剧情其实包含两个不同的视角。

00:25:56.019 --> 00:26:00.059
Yeah, and that's true of a lot of really good Hemium episodes.
是的，许多非常棒的《老爸老妈浪漫史》单集都是这样。

00:26:00.059 --> 00:26:03.099
There's not quite a B store, there's kind of things to play along the way.
没有完全独立的B线，而是在主线推进的过程中穿插一些小插曲。

00:26:03.099 --> 00:26:07.660
And I really like lilies in this, but you're right, everyone's kind of on the same train.
我很喜欢莉莉在这集里的表现，但你说得对，大家基本上都在同一列火车上。

00:26:07.660 --> 00:26:13.299
There's one train creating the momentum, and then there's these little things going on for people that are on that one train.
有一列火车在推动整体势头，然后这列火车上的人又各自经历着一些小插曲。

00:26:13.299 --> 00:26:22.140
Lilies I liked a lot, because Lilies was Robin, I'm teaching you about what it takes to be in a relationship, the sacrifices, the listening to the complaints.
我非常喜欢莉莉的部分，因为莉莉扮演了罗宾的导师，在教她经营一段感情需要付出什么，要懂得牺牲，要学会倾听抱怨。

00:26:22.140 --> 00:26:28.299
And I know this and I don't want to see you fuck it up because I really miss Marshall and I lost that.
“我知道这些，我不想看你把事情搞砸，因为我真的很想念马修，而我失去了这一切。”

00:26:28.299 --> 00:26:29.700
And now I wish I had that back.
“现在我多么希望当初能拥有它。”

00:26:29.700 --> 00:26:30.700
So don't lose it.
“所以千万别弄丢了。”

00:26:30.700 --> 00:26:32.980
That was a good angle for Lilly.
对莉莉来说，这个切入点很好。

00:26:32.980 --> 00:26:34.579
And that's kind of enough.
而且这样就已经足够了。

00:26:34.579 --> 00:26:36.099
It's a 22 minute thing.
总共也就22分钟的时长。

00:26:36.099 --> 00:26:43.420
Sometimes when we did A story, B story, C stories or D story, it gets so hard to keep all those plates spinning.
有时当我们同时处理A线、B线、C线甚至D线时，要让所有这些盘子都转起来真的很难。

00:26:43.420 --> 00:26:50.980
When you find these ideas where everything is on that one train and there's one main thing, it's kind of great.
当你找到这种所有内容都集中在同一列火车上、只有一个核心主题的点子时，那就太棒了。

00:26:50.980 --> 00:26:53.000
And this one had that momentum.
而这一集就很有这种推进感。

00:26:53.000 --> 00:26:57.359
Yeah, I always like, I think Alison's very good at being like relationship Yoda.
对，我一直很喜欢，我觉得艾莉森非常擅长扮演情感方面的“尤达大师”。

00:26:57.359 --> 00:26:59.480
Yeah, but she's bruised as she's doing it.
对，但她在这么做的时候自己内心其实伤痕累累。

00:26:59.480 --> 00:27:02.880
She's lost Marshall and I liked that so much in this one.
她失去了马修，而我非常喜欢这一集里对这一点的刻画。

00:27:03.880 --> 00:27:09.000
And it kind of catches Robin's attention when she says, Hey, I wish I had this right now.
当她说出“嘿，我现在真希望我能拥有这一切”时，确实引起了罗宾的注意。

00:27:09.000 --> 00:27:10.480
Yeah, what I wouldn't give.
是的，我简直愿意付出一切。

00:27:10.480 --> 00:27:22.920
Yeah, I did think it was a funny runner of Marshall thinking, oh, my law school classmates are gonna be I was so devastated by the work of this great guy.
对，我觉得马修脑子里那种想法挺搞笑的，他觉得：“哦，我的法学院同学一定会为这个伟大地家伙的遭遇感到痛心疾首。”

00:27:22.920 --> 00:27:25.279
But you know, I had this in my 20s.
但你知道的，我在20多岁的时候也有过这种经历。

00:27:25.279 --> 00:27:29.079
I had a three year relationship with this wonderful woman.
我和这个优秀的女人谈了三年恋爱。

00:27:29.079 --> 00:27:39.119
And I somehow, because it was my first like, very big long-term relationship, I thought everyone was very invested in us being together and staying together, very invested.
某种程度上，因为这是我的第一段非常深刻的长期恋爱，我以为所有人都极其关注我们是否能在一起、是否能长久，极其关注。

00:27:39.119 --> 00:27:39.960
You know?
你知道吗？

00:27:39.960 --> 00:27:41.359
This is the glue holding the universe together.
这就是维系宇宙的纽带。

00:27:41.359 --> 00:27:43.839
Yeah, at least, at least the west side of Los Angeles.
对，至少……至少对洛杉矶西区来说是这样的。

00:27:43.839 --> 00:27:46.880
I thought they need us to be together.
我以为他们需要我们在一起。

00:27:46.880 --> 00:27:52.160
And when we broke up, I had this thing, what are our friends gonna think this is?
当我们分手的时候，我心里一直在想：我们的朋友会怎么想？

00:27:52.160 --> 00:27:53.240
How do I break this news?
我该怎么开口宣布这个消息？

00:27:53.240 --> 00:27:54.640
How do I break this news?
我该怎么把这消息说出口？

00:27:54.640 --> 00:28:01.640
When I tell you that no one was shocked and no one was particularly thrown, everyone was like, yeah, I kind of saw that.
可当我告诉你时，根本没有人震惊，也没有人觉得特别意外，大家都是一副“哦，我早就看出来了”的表情。

00:28:01.640 --> 00:28:03.000
I didn't think you guys would.
“我本来就不觉得你们能走到最后。”

00:28:03.000 --> 00:28:04.079
Do you wanna play tennis?
“你想去打网球吗？”

00:28:04.079 --> 00:28:07.920
Like, no one had a problem with it.
大家都觉得这根本不算事儿。

00:28:07.920 --> 00:28:12.759
And it was just a reminder of how we naturally center ourselves.
这只是又一次提醒了我们，人是多么习惯于以自我为中心。

00:28:12.759 --> 00:28:15.960
Yes, Marshall was in his own.
没错，马修当时正沉浸在自己的世界里。

00:28:15.960 --> 00:28:17.799
He's just like the main character.
他觉得自己就像是主角。

00:28:17.799 --> 00:28:28.160
And he's the main character, him and Lily, were the glue that was holding this law school class together, this Columbia law school class was like, needed Lily and Marshall to be together.
他觉得自己是主角，他和莉莉是维系整个法学院班级的纽带，这个哥伦比亚法学院的班级仿佛……离不开莉莉和马修这对情侣。

00:28:28.160 --> 00:28:29.559
And it's, yeah.
确实是这样。

00:28:29.559 --> 00:28:32.519
He even says like, we're the couple that made you all believe in love.
他甚至还说：“我们是让你们所有人重新相信爱情的情侣。”

00:28:32.519 --> 00:28:33.599
And I'm just like, what?
而我当时的反应是：什么鬼？

00:28:35.549 --> 00:28:40.029
But back actually pointed out, watching it again, she's like, shouldn't Brad have said like, hey, Lily, what's up?
但贝克重看的时候其实指出了这一点，她说：“布拉德难道不应该说：‘嘿，莉莉，最近怎么样？’之类的话吗？”

00:28:40.029 --> 00:28:43.869
Cause Lily comes knocking and it's Brad and she's, all those people are supposed to know her and stuff.
因为莉莉去敲门，开门的是布拉德，而且剧里那些人理应都是认识她的。

00:28:43.869 --> 00:28:44.750
And no one really acknowledges her.
但根本没有人真正理会她。

00:28:44.750 --> 00:28:45.710
It kind of felt like an error.
感觉这有点像是个失误。

00:28:45.710 --> 00:28:49.509
Like if I could go back, I think I would have had Brad be like, hey, Lily's heard about the breakup.
如果能重新来过，我想我会让布拉德说：“嘿，莉莉，听说你和马修分手了。”

00:28:49.549 --> 00:28:51.109
Like some acknowledgement, I don't know.
诸如此类表示认可的话，我不知道。

00:28:51.109 --> 00:28:55.670
Maybe we had that in the script or shot it and cut it and added because we were always so overly long.
也许我们在剧本里写过，或者拍了之后剪掉了，因为我们的时长总是严重超标。

00:28:55.670 --> 00:28:56.910
But it was a good point.
但这确实是个好观点。

00:28:56.910 --> 00:28:58.829
It was like, that was a little bit of a blooper.
这就有点像是个小穿帮。

00:28:59.190 --> 00:29:03.309
It almost felt like someone should have just been like, hey, Lily, you're a marshaller both here.
感觉应该有个人走过来说一句：“嘿，莉莉，你和马修都在这儿啊。”

00:29:03.309 --> 00:29:03.789
Is that okay?
“这样没问题吧？”

00:29:03.869 --> 00:29:06.509
Like some, some little moment like that probably should have been in there.
诸如此类的小互动，大概应该加进去才对。

00:29:06.589 --> 00:29:09.470
I like to do me a couple of us as we go and just own some of these.
我喜欢边看边拿我们自己开涮，主动承认这些剧中的瑕疵。

00:29:10.910 --> 00:29:16.869
Some of them are we offended a marginalized community and otherwise other ones are but we made a narrative misstep.
有些瑕疵是我们冒犯了某个边缘化群体，而另一些则是我们在叙事上出现了失误。

00:29:16.869 --> 00:29:18.000
Heh heh heh.
呵呵呵。

